# Autonomous Trading System - Core Rules
# Canonical guidance lives in .cursor/rules/. This file is the legacy-compatible
# mirror of .cursor/rules/00-core.mdc for tools that read only .cursorrules.

## AUTHORITY

`docs/context/` is canonical. It defines a four-layer architecture in which
deterministic code owns every live decision.

`docs/research/` holds three superseded design documents. They are historical
input, kept for provenance. Never implement from them. In particular the
Service-1/2/3 plus Agent-1/2/3/4 topology, the AI-in-the-loop order-flow
detector, the CAS naked-call lottery trade, Redis as position authority, and
every hard-coded SEBI threshold in those documents are rejected designs.

## NON-NEGOTIABLE INVARIANTS

1. Deterministic versioned code exclusively controls live signals, sizing,
   allocation, risk, order creation, stops, exits and flattening.
2. AI is asynchronous and proposal-only. It never calls a broker, mutates live
   state or config, or bypasses a gate. No LLM call in any live decision path.
3. Strategies emit `TradeIntent` only. They never construct broker orders.
4. Layer 2 recalculates every financially authoritative value at decision time.
5. Broker orders, fills, positions and funds are external truth.
6. Unknown, stale, inconsistent or invalid critical state blocks new exposure.
   Existing positions keep deterministic protection regardless.
7. One logical order uses one stable idempotency key across every retry. A
   timeout never proves a fill. Unknown outcome blocks replacement until
   reconciliation.
8. Every open position maps to active deterministic protective coverage. Stops
   only tighten.

Full list: `docs/context/SAFETY_INVARIANTS.md`. Any change touching an
invariant requires an explicit failure test.

## LAYER OWNERSHIP

| Layer | Owns | Never |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 Data | Feeds, quality state, features, IV/Greeks, replay | Trade selection, sizing |
| 2 Risk | Portfolio, sizing, limits, OMS, exits, reconciliation | Setup detection |
| 3 Strategy | Setup logic, requested risk, exit template | Final qty, broker calls |
| 4 Analytics | Evaluation, proposals, backtest, shadow | Any live mutation |

```text
External data -> L1 FeatureSnapshot -> L3 TradeIntent
              -> L2 RiskDecision/OMS -> Broker
Broker events -> L2 reconciliation/portfolio/trade management
Audit evidence -> L4 evaluation -> proposal -> promotion gate
```

No arrow goes directly from AI or a strategy to the broker.

Domain and calculation code imports no infrastructure. Side effects live behind
typed ports. Modular monolith first.

## CONFIGURATION, NOT CONSTANTS

Lot sizes, order-rate caps, margin composition rules, expiry penalties, loss
caps, position limits and freshness thresholds are validated configuration in
`config/`. Never a literal in domain logic. Never a remembered value: verify
against current official documentation before use. Unverified config fails
closed rather than defaulting.

## MONEY AND TIME

`Decimal` or integer minor units for anything financial; `float` never reaches
an accounting or order-validation path. All timestamps timezone-aware, stored
UTC, with exchange-local session logic. Time and IDs are injected via `Clock`
and `IdFactory`, never read from ambient global state.

## STORAGE

Order, trade and configuration state requires a transactional store with
durable commits. Polars for tabular calculation; DuckDB and Parquet for
research and replay. JSONL is an append-only audit export, not the
authoritative record. Redis only where coordination or caching is justified,
and never as position authority.

## TESTING

Test boundaries and failure paths, not the happy path. Tests run offline and
deterministically: no network, no wall clock, no ambient randomness. Name the
safety invariant a test covers in its docstring.

## STYLE

Python 3.12, type hints mandatory, `mypy --strict` clean, `ruff` formatted.
Docstrings one line. Comments explain why, not what. Patch minimally; never
reformat untouched code. Reuse existing patterns instead of rewriting them.
